Linear vectorization is supported only for C and Go with the simple and record
APIs. Other languages and the generic API reject both the command-line option
and the configuration. For one-byte target encodings in Go, ``re2c:YYCTYPE``
must be the name ``byte`` or ``uint8``; other type names are rejected, including
user-defined aliases. The generated fast path also contains a check that the Go
compiler resolves, and the generated file needs no import. The check indexes a
one-element array with the length of an array whose length is
``int(^uint8(0))>>8+int(^YYCTYPE(0))>>8``. The check depends on ``YYCTYPE``
and assumes that ``int``, ``uint8`` and ``byte``, as visible from the lexer,
are the predeclared Go types; a declaration that redefines one of them, in the
package or locally, is outside this contract. A wider
resolution is out of bounds; a signed resolution gives a negative array length.
Either failure rejects the generated file when ``byte`` and ``uint8`` are
ordinary named types or aliases. For the predeclared one-byte types the index is
zero, the check is a constant, and it does not remain in the machine code. A
generic type parameter, including one named ``byte`` or ``uint8``, makes the
array length non-constant, so the generated file is rejected at compilation
instead of panicking at run time.

The input may be a string or a slice. A wider slice element, such as
``[]uint16`` or ``[]rune``, is accepted when the names ``byte`` and ``uint8``
are real one-byte types. Each element is then truncated to its low 8 bits by
``uint8``, the same
truncation a scalar ``YYCTYPE`` conversion applies. The compiler check does not
measure the slice element, so this truncation stays available. A one-byte signed
element such as ``[]int8`` is truncated the same way, without sign extension.

With ``--vectorize-linear`` (or ``re2c:vectorize:linear`` configuration) |re2c|
looks for *linear chains* in the DFA: sequences of states where every state has
exactly one single-character transition that continues the chain. Such chains
appear when the input is dispatched on long fixed strings, for example HTTP
header names, tag or attribute names, or file signatures. At the first state of a
chain the generated lexer assembles the next 2, 4 or 8 code units into a value and
compares them with the characters on the chain. If they are equal, it skips them
and jumps to the last state of the chain; otherwise it continues with the
ordinary one-character dispatch. The fast path is taken only when the ordinary
dispatch would follow the same transitions, so the matched rule, the match
length and the cursor are the same as without the option.

The option can help when the lexer spends its time on long literals that are
often matched to the end, for example when classifying isolated known header
names along linear paths. It is not a general keyword-recognition optimization:
with an identifier rule such as ``[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*``, every prefix of a
keyword is an accepting state, and the chain stops there. On short tokens or
unknown names that rarely match a literal, the extra comparison may cost more
than it saves.

There is no universal speedup for C lexers using ``YYFILL``. Comparing isolated
names with a refilled stream that also scans header values changes several
factors at once. The benchmark suite separates these workloads and compares
resident input with checks disabled, resident input with checks enabled, and
actual refills using the same checked scanner (see
``benchmarks/vectorize-linear``).
Benchmark the actual lexer, input distribution and buffer contract rather than
assuming that fewer character comparisons imply lower running time.

The multi-character primitives are defined by the following code templates in
the syntax file (see the section about syntax files):

``code:yypeekn``
    An expression that yields the value of the ``n`` code units at the cursor,
    where the code unit at offset ``k`` occupies bits ``8*k`` to ``8*k+7``. The
    literal that the value is compared with uses the same layout, so the value
    does not depend on the byte order of the host. Supported variables: ``n``,
    ``YYINPUT``, ``YYCURSOR``, ``yyrecord``, and the list variable ``offset``
    that enumerates code unit offsets from ``0`` to ``n-1``.

``code:yypeekn_guard``
    A condition that must hold before ``code:yypeekn`` is evaluated, or an empty
    string if no check is needed. Supported variables: ``n``, ``YYCTYPE``,
    ``YYINPUT``, ``YYCURSOR``, ``YYLIMIT``, ``yyrecord``.

``code:yyskipn``
    A statement that advances the cursor by ``n`` code units. Here ``n`` is
    the number of units to skip, not the length of the chain: it is one less
    than the length, because the state after the chain consumes a unit on
    entry, or the full length with ``--eager-skip``. Supported variables:
    ``n``, ``YYINPUT``, ``YYCURSOR``, ``yyrecord``.

The C and Go syntax files define them for the simple and record APIs. The C
definitions assemble the value in an ``unsigned long long`` (C99, C++11); for
C90 or C++98 redefine ``code:yypeekn`` in a custom syntax file, or leave the
option disabled.
The value is assembled from single bytes of the input. Combining these
byte reads into one machine load depends on the compiler, optimization flags
and surrounding code; it is not guaranteed. In C a ``sizeof(*YYCURSOR) == 1``
guard restricts this fast path to one-byte input elements. In Go the
corresponding check is the compile-time width test described above. Both with
and without ``YYFILL`` the reads are also guarded by a bounds check, so that a
``YYFILL`` which does not provide the requested input (for example a no-op
``YYFILL`` in a sentinel lexer without ``YYMAXFILL`` padding) cannot make the
fast path read past the end of the input: the fast path is skipped and the
ordinary dispatch runs instead. In C the check uses ``YYLIMIT``, which must
point after the last readable code unit. If ``YYFILL`` or its check is
disabled, the C simple and record APIs require explicit configuration of this
limit (for example, ``re2c:YYLIMIT = YYLIMIT;`` for the simple API or
``re2c:YYLIMIT = "yyrecord->yylimit";`` for a record field); otherwise linear
vectorization is disabled with a warning and sentinel-only lexers need no limit
declaration. In Go the check uses ``YYLIMIT`` when ``YYFILL`` is enabled and the
length of ``YYINPUT`` otherwise.

The optimization applies only to target encodings with one-byte code units,
including ASCII and UTF-8. For wider target encodings it is disabled with a
warning. These warnings are controlled by ``-Wvectorize-linear-disabled``.
It is also disabled with a warning when ``re2c:eof`` is configured,
because then ``YYFILL`` does not reserve input in advance. The
end-of-input rule ``$`` alone does not disable it: ``$`` can also use ``YYEND``
with the usual ``YYMAXFILL`` padding contract. Chains do not pass through
accepting states or states with tag operations, ``YYFILL``
checks or backtracking save points, but they may end in such a state, for
example in the accepting state of a literal.
